 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot V)$ per frame, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray and $V$ is the number of rays (pixels). The DFF query adds a constant overhead per sample. It is significantly faster than optimization-based methods which require iterative gradient descent [S1, 12372-nerf-in-inpainting-optimization.md].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the NeRF weights and the DFF weights. No additional memory for optimization buffers or large feature grids is needed during inference [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance; view consistency is inherent due to the 3D nature of the DFF and NeRF; no retraining required [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Quality depends heavily on the accuracy of the DFF and the NeRF [S1]. Geometric edits (translation) can cause artifacts at boundaries if the mixing strategy is not robust [S1]. Semantic segmentation errors in the DFF lead to incorrect selection [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time or near-real-time editing is required, and the scene structure is well-captured by the initial NeRF [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When the edit requires significant geometric changes that break the underlying NeRF's representation (e.g., adding new objects not present in the scene) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The editing step adds a constant overhead per sample point for the DFF query and potential inverse transformation lookup. It avoids the iterative gradient descent required by optimization-based methods, making inference significantly faster per frame once the DFF is distilled [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the distilled Feature Field (DFF) or teacher network features in addition to the NeRF weights [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Immediate application of edits; supports semantic (colorization) and geometric (translation/deletion) edits simultaneously; no retraining of the NeRF backbone required [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Quality depends on the accuracy of the DFF/teacher network; geometric edits may suffer from aliasing or artifacts at boundaries if the transformation is not smooth [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Editing Procedure was introduced in the paper "Decomposing NeRF for Editing Via Feature Field Distillation" (DFF) [S1]. It builds on the concept of distilling semantic information from large pre-trained models (like LSeg or DINO) into a lightweight feature field that can be queried alongside the NeRF [S1]. This allows for semantic-aware editing without retraining the NeRF itself [S1].
+This procedure originates from the work on Decomposing NeRF for Editing Via Feature Field Distillation (DFF) [S1]. It leverages pre-trained teacher networks like LSeg and DINO to distill semantic and geometric information into a field that guides the editing of the radiance field [S1].
 
